-- §5  Which buffers the corpus sizes, and which of them read zero.
# One row per (buffer, evidence) from the slack census over every filing: sized, sized at zero,
# absent, and the total.
#
# Sized zero against unmeasured
#   A sized zero and an unmeasured buffer are opposite claims, and this splits them. Corpus layers
#   that size their capacity slack size it at [0,0,0], a filer saying the supply cannot stretch.
#   That is the tightest possible bound, not a missing one.
#
# Prose about this number goes stale
#   It is wrong as soon as the corpus moves, and it moves. Sentences elsewhere once said no corpus
#   layer sizes a capacity slack while several did; neither was lying when written, and no program
#   read them. That is what this whole example is for.
-- reports/slack_census.sqlc over every filing, projected for the example.
SELECT c.buffer         AS "buffer!",
       c.evidence       AS "evidence",
       c.sized          AS "sized!",
       c.sized_at_zero  AS "sized_at_zero!",
       c.absent         AS "absent!",
       c.rows           AS "rows!"
FROM (
    :compose(reports/slack_census.sqlc, @scope = scope/every_filing.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.buffer, c.evidence
